id: pm-ag9nka
title: "The --profile timer reports a fraction of real invocation latency and is documented nowhere, so every latency judgement built on it is calibrated against the wrong number"
description: "pm exposes a global --profile flag described in root help as 'Print deterministic timing diagnostics'. Fifty-eight call sites emit a single line, profile:command=<name> took_ms=<n>, computed as Date.now() minus a start timestamp taken inside the command handler. That span excludes process start, module graph resolution, settings and schema load, extension loading, and output rendering, which together are the majority of what a caller actually waits for.\n\nMeasured on this repository's live tracker (2,037 items), dist build 2026.7.25, wall clock from the shell against the number the same invocation printed:\n\n  pm list --limit 5              wall 1004 ms   profile 200 ms    20 percent reported\n  pm get pm-doxj                 wall  882 ms   profile 286 ms    32 percent\n  pm graph audit                 wall 1470 ms   profile 521 ms    35 percent\n  pm next --limit 3              wall 1307 ms   profile 577 ms    44 percent\n  pm search context --limit 5    wall 1539 ms   profile 602 ms    39 percent\n  pm context --limit 5           wall 1410 ms   profile 684 ms    49 percent\n  pm stats                       wall 1887 ms   profile 1155 ms   61 percent\n  pm validate                    wall 8135 ms   profile 7200 ms   89 percent\n\nThe un-instrumented remainder is roughly 600 to 800 ms and is close to constant, so the instrument is least truthful exactly where the product most wants to be fast. On pm list it hides four fifths of the cost. An agent or maintainer using --profile to decide whether a command is fast reads 200 ms for a one-second wait, and any budget or ratchet built on the printed value would pass while the experienced latency regressed.\n\nSecond half: --profile appears in no file under docs/. It is a global flag on every command with no reference documentation, no statement of what span it measures, and no note that the number is not end-to-end. There is nothing to correct the natural reading of took_ms as total command time.\n\nScope boundary. pm-yse5dt owns the per-invocation bootstrap floor and the CLI-versus-SDK delta as gated metrics; pm-801d owns read scaling; pm-9rxu owns the scale program. This item is about the measuring instrument rather than the thing measured: those items need a truthful timer to gate against, and today the only built-in one systematically under-reports."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[5]: agent-ux,"area:cli",diagnostics,observability,performance
created_at: "2026-07-25T21:38:06.243Z"
